# Kotlin/Android testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' testing rules to `kotlinx-coroutines-test`, Compose
UI testing and mocking on Android.

## Coroutine test dispatchers

- Test a `suspend` function or `ViewModel` coroutine behavior with `runTest { }`,
  not a real `delay` or blocking sleep.
- Inject a `TestDispatcher` (`StandardTestDispatcher`/`UnconfinedTestDispatcher`)
  in place of `Dispatchers.Main`/`Dispatchers.IO`; `Dispatchers.setMain(testDispatcher)`
  in `@Before` and `Dispatchers.resetMain()` in `@After` (or a JUnit rule).
- `StandardTestDispatcher` (advance with `advanceUntilIdle()`/`runCurrent()`) when
  a test asserts an intermediate state; `UnconfinedTestDispatcher` when only the
  end state matters.
- Never synchronize a coroutine test with `Thread.sleep` or a fixed `delay`.

## Compose UI testing

- Drive assertions through a `ComposeTestRule` (`createComposeRule()`, or
  `createAndroidComposeRule<Activity>()` when a hosting Activity is needed);
  never reach into the `View` tree.
- Find nodes with `onNodeWithText`, `onNodeWithContentDescription`,
  `onNodeWithTag(testTag)`, not structural/index-based queries; add
  `Modifier.testTag("...")` where there is no stable text or content description.
- Wait for async UI with `composeTestRule.waitUntil { ... }`, `awaitIdle()` or
  `mainClock.advanceTimeUntil { }`, not a fixed sleep.
- Assert structure and behavior: presence/text, and an interaction's effect
  (`performClick()` then the resulting state), not just that it rendered.

## Mocking at the interface boundary

- Mock a `Repository`/`ApiService`/`DataSource` interface or other clearly-owned
  collaborator boundary; never stub the class under test's own private methods or fields.
- Prefer MockK for Kotlin code, unless the project has standardized on Mockito
  with `mockito-kotlin`; match the project, do not add a second mocking library.
- `coEvery { repo.fetch() } returns result` / `coVerify { repo.fetch() }` for a
  suspend function on a mock, not `every`/`verify`.
- Fake network/database responses at the repository or data-source interface in
  unit tests; a real backing store belongs in an instrumented/integration test.

## Layout and lifecycle

- `ViewModel`/plain Kotlin unit tests under `src/test/`; tests needing an Android
  context or device (Compose UI, `Instrumentation`) under `src/androidTest/`;
  match the project's layout, no third location.
- Use JUnit rules (`MainDispatcherRule`, `createComposeRule()`) for dispatcher and
  UI setup/teardown.
